  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 206 AM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nOverall, sensible weather does not change much day to day in the \nlong term period. Hot temperatures are very likely to remain with \nhighs looking to drop just a few degrees as we go into the middle of \nnext week. Daily thunderstorm chances will remain through the next \nseveral days at least, if not extending further past the end of the \nlong term period. Models are still pinging an increase in moisture \ncoming into the area, as evident by the gradual increase in PWAT \nvalues across the state. Southerly components of 700mb flow look to \nincrease during this timeframe, which would be the catalyst (or at \nleast one factor) of an increased surge in moisture. This flow has a \ndecent chance to be affected by some approaching easterly waves, \nwhich are very hard to narrow down this far out. With the good \nconsistency through the last several model runs (over the course of \na few days now), precipitation chances do begin to rise as we get \ninto the early to middle part of next week. What does that mean for \nthe overall precipitation for New Mexico? That remains to be \nseen, especially for specific locations. This pattern does favor \nmore precipitation returning to areas in western and northern NM \nthat have missed out on rainfall over the past few days, which is \na promising sign. Nonetheless, this pattern still speaks as a \n\"wait and see\" type for where the true beneficial rainfall occurs \nand how location-specific Monsoon rainfall amounts turn out.",
